1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,628
You paid the jeweller for gold.

2
00:00:01,628 --> 00:00:04,340
So how much gold actually came home? Not the bill,

3
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:07,053
not the box. The real metal you can sell tomorrow.

4
00:00:07,053 --> 00:00:09,805
Most families never ask this question. The jeweller smiles,

5
00:00:09,805 --> 00:00:11,640
and the bill shows the total.

6
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:14,087
Everyone believes all of it went into gold.

7
00:00:14,087 --> 00:00:15,452
But here is the twist.

8
00:00:15,452 --> 00:00:17,908
A real slice of that money never becomes gold.

9
00:00:17,908 --> 00:00:19,818
It goes to making charges and GST,

10
00:00:19,818 --> 00:00:21,183
on the very first day.

11
00:00:21,183 --> 00:00:23,969
And the day you sell, that part simply disappears.

12
00:00:23,969 --> 00:00:26,446
Many jewellers pay you only for the metal.

13
00:00:26,446 --> 00:00:29,232
The rest is gone forever, and almost nobody notices.

14
00:00:29,232 --> 00:00:32,267
So is jewellery a bad buy? Not at all.

15
00:00:32,267 --> 00:00:34,965
A wedding necklace is love, tradition and pride.

16
00:00:34,965 --> 00:00:38,000
But is it also a smart way to save?

17
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,804
Wedding season has just started after Dev Uthani.

18
00:00:40,804 --> 00:00:44,309
Families across India are walking into jewellery shops right now.

19
00:00:44,309 --> 00:00:46,763
This question touches almost every Indian home.

20
00:00:46,763 --> 00:00:48,114
Today, 3 questions. First,

21
00:00:48,114 --> 00:00:51,490
where does your money actually go on the bill? Then,

22
00:00:51,490 --> 00:00:54,529
what do you really get back when you sell?

23
00:00:54,529 --> 00:00:58,472
And last, if gold is savings, is there a smarter way?

24
00:00:58,472 --> 00:01:02,057
Coin, jewellery or a gold ETF? Stay till the end,

25
00:01:02,057 --> 00:01:03,849
the answer surprises most people.

26
00:01:03,849 --> 00:01:06,352
Picture a mother in a busy jewellery market.

27
00:01:06,352 --> 00:01:09,482
Her daughter's wedding is coming soon. She has saved slowly,

28
00:01:09,482 --> 00:01:11,672
over many months, for a single necklace.

29
00:01:11,672 --> 00:01:13,346
She picks a design she loves.

30
00:01:13,346 --> 00:01:15,857
The jeweller weighs it and checks today's gold rate.

31
00:01:15,857 --> 00:01:18,368
Then he starts writing the bill, line by line.

32
00:01:18,368 --> 00:01:20,505
The first line is the gold itself.

33
00:01:20,505 --> 00:01:22,032
Weight multiplied by today's rate.

34
00:01:22,032 --> 00:01:24,780
This is the only part that is real metal,

35
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:26,001
the part you own.

36
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:27,698
Next comes the making charge.

37
00:01:27,698 --> 00:01:31,093
That is the fee for the craftsman's work: cutting, shaping,

38
00:01:31,093 --> 00:01:33,470
polishing and design. Here is the catch.

39
00:01:33,470 --> 00:01:35,167
There is no fixed rate.

40
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:37,046
Some jewellers charge it per gram.

41
00:01:37,046 --> 00:01:40,178
Others charge a percentage of the gold value. A fine,

42
00:01:40,178 --> 00:01:43,623
detailed necklace costs far more to make than a plain chain.

43
00:01:43,623 --> 00:01:46,921
And notice this. The making charge buys you beauty, not gold.

44
00:01:46,921 --> 00:01:49,919
Not a single gram of metal comes from that money.

45
00:01:49,919 --> 00:01:50,819
Remember this line.

46
00:01:50,819 --> 00:01:53,419
Then comes GST. On the value of the gold,

47
00:01:53,419 --> 00:01:56,307
GST is 3 percent. The GST Council fixed this rate,

48
00:01:56,307 --> 00:01:58,618
and it applies to coins and bars too.

49
00:01:58,618 --> 00:02:01,628
But the making charge has its own GST. As reported,

50
00:02:01,628 --> 00:02:03,434
it is 5 percent, charged separately.

51
00:02:03,434 --> 00:02:06,143
So you pay tax on the craftsman's fee too.

52
00:02:06,143 --> 00:02:09,168
Add it all up and you get the final bill.

53
00:02:09,168 --> 00:02:11,891
Now ask the real question, the one nobody asks.

54
00:02:11,891 --> 00:02:14,009
How much of that is actually gold?

55
00:02:14,009 --> 00:02:17,872
Here is the answer. Only the first line. The gold value.

56
00:02:17,872 --> 00:02:21,736
Everything else, the making charge and both GST lines, is cost,

57
00:02:21,736 --> 00:02:22,438
not metal.

58
00:02:22,438 --> 00:02:25,164
On a plain chain, the cost part stays small.

59
00:02:25,164 --> 00:02:26,981
On a heavy, detailed bridal set,

60
00:02:26,981 --> 00:02:29,707
it can eat a big slice of your savings.

61
00:02:29,707 --> 00:02:32,888
Necklaces of the same weight can carry very different bills.

62
00:02:32,888 --> 00:02:35,751
The gold inside is equal. The craftsmanship is not,

63
00:02:35,751 --> 00:02:37,659
and you pay for that difference.

64
00:02:37,659 --> 00:02:39,545
Think of it like a restaurant meal.

65
00:02:39,545 --> 00:02:41,971
You pay for the food, but also the chef,

66
00:02:41,971 --> 00:02:44,935
the service and the tax. Only the food fills your stomach.

67
00:02:44,935 --> 00:02:46,782
Jewellery works the same way.

68
00:02:46,782 --> 00:02:50,846
You enjoy the craftsman's work for years, at weddings and festivals.

69
00:02:50,846 --> 00:02:53,432
For a family, that has real value.

70
00:02:53,432 --> 00:02:56,775
But money-wise, only the gold travels with you into the future.

71
00:02:56,775 --> 00:02:58,599
So what happens when you sell?

72
00:02:58,599 --> 00:03:00,726
This is where the real shock begins.

73
00:03:00,726 --> 00:03:02,190
Fast forward a few years.

74
00:03:02,190 --> 00:03:04,532
The family needs money for a hospital bill,

75
00:03:04,532 --> 00:03:05,996
or a child's college fees.

76
00:03:05,996 --> 00:03:08,046
The necklace goes back to the shop.

77
00:03:08,046 --> 00:03:11,184
The jeweller weighs it again and tests the purity.

78
00:03:11,184 --> 00:03:14,670
Then he checks today's gold rate and makes an offer.

79
00:03:14,670 --> 00:03:16,065
The mother waits, hopeful.

80
00:03:16,065 --> 00:03:19,031
Usually, the offer is only for the gold inside.

81
00:03:19,031 --> 00:03:21,008
The making charge she paid? Gone.

82
00:03:21,008 --> 00:03:23,645
The GST on that making charge? Also gone.

83
00:03:23,645 --> 00:03:25,638
And the 3 percent GST on the gold?

84
00:03:25,638 --> 00:03:27,134
That does not come back either.

85
00:03:27,134 --> 00:03:30,373
Tax paid on a purchase is not refunded to you when you sell.

86
00:03:30,373 --> 00:03:33,904
So the cost part of your bill vanished on the very first day.

87
00:03:33,904 --> 00:03:36,892
The moment you walked out of the shop, it was lost.

88
00:03:36,892 --> 00:03:38,824
There can be another cut too.

89
00:03:38,824 --> 00:03:42,366
Some jewellers deduct a small amount for melting or purity loss.

90
00:03:42,366 --> 00:03:44,942
Ask about this before you buy, not after.

91
00:03:44,942 --> 00:03:47,186
Now, the gold price may rise over those years.

92
00:03:47,186 --> 00:03:48,683
That rise can cover the loss.

93
00:03:48,683 --> 00:03:52,174
But first it must rise just to get you back to where you started.

94
00:03:52,174 --> 00:03:53,818
That is the hidden trap.

95
00:03:53,818 --> 00:03:58,094
Your jewellery must climb a hill before it even reaches the starting line.

96
00:03:58,094 --> 00:04:00,067
A plain coin starts much closer.

97
00:04:00,067 --> 00:04:01,948
But wait, selling has a second risk.

98
00:04:01,948 --> 00:04:05,171
What if the gold is not as pure as the bill said?

99
00:04:05,171 --> 00:04:07,052
Who decides the purity at the counter?

100
00:04:07,052 --> 00:04:09,892
For years, old family jewellery carried no proof of purity.

101
00:04:09,892 --> 00:04:11,029
The buyer tested it,

102
00:04:11,029 --> 00:04:13,585
and the family simply had to trust his word.

103
00:04:13,585 --> 00:04:15,223
This is why hallmarking matters.

104
00:04:15,223 --> 00:04:18,499
Every hallmarked piece now carries a unique code called HUID.

105
00:04:18,499 --> 00:04:21,448
It works like an Aadhaar number for your jewellery.

106
00:04:21,448 --> 00:04:24,724
Hallmarking is run by BIS, the Bureau of Indian Standards.

107
00:04:24,724 --> 00:04:29,311
An approved centre tests the gold and stamps its purity right on the piece.

108
00:04:29,311 --> 00:04:32,333
Hallmarking is now mandatory, and the scale is huge.

109
00:04:32,333 --> 00:04:35,356
Over 65 crore gold pieces have received this code.

110
00:04:35,356 --> 00:04:38,378
That is the government's count, as of this October.

111
00:04:38,378 --> 00:04:41,438
So before you pay, look for these marks on the piece.

112
00:04:41,438 --> 00:04:44,221
The BIS logo, the purity grade, and the HUID code.

113
00:04:44,221 --> 00:04:45,890
Match the code with your bill.

114
00:04:45,890 --> 00:04:48,877
You can check the HUID yourself on the BIS Care app.

115
00:04:48,877 --> 00:04:51,865
It shows the purity and the jeweller who registered that piece.

116
00:04:51,865 --> 00:04:53,358
A hallmark protects your purity.

117
00:04:53,358 --> 00:04:55,745
But it does not protect your making charge.

118
00:04:55,745 --> 00:04:58,432
That money is still lost the day you sell.

119
00:04:58,432 --> 00:05:00,609
So if you buy gold only to save,

120
00:05:00,609 --> 00:05:02,786
is there a smarter way to hold it?

121
00:05:02,786 --> 00:05:04,691
Here is where most people get surprised.

122
00:05:04,691 --> 00:05:06,421
Picture a brother and a sister.

123
00:05:06,421 --> 00:05:09,305
Both get a bonus at work, and both want gold.

124
00:05:09,305 --> 00:05:12,189
She wants to wear it. He only wants to save.

125
00:05:12,189 --> 00:05:13,878
For the sister, jewellery makes sense.

126
00:05:13,878 --> 00:05:16,411
She will wear it at every wedding and festival.

127
00:05:16,411 --> 00:05:19,225
The making charge is the price of beauty she enjoys.

128
00:05:19,225 --> 00:05:22,352
But for the brother, the making charge is pure leakage.

129
00:05:22,352 --> 00:05:25,791
Leakage means money that drips out and never becomes an asset.

130
00:05:25,791 --> 00:05:27,354
So what are his choices?

131
00:05:27,354 --> 00:05:30,132
First, a gold coin or a small bar. Plain metal,

132
00:05:30,132 --> 00:05:31,522
with very little design work.

133
00:05:31,522 --> 00:05:34,578
So the making cost is usually much lower than on jewellery.

134
00:05:34,578 --> 00:05:37,772
But a coin still pays the same GST on the gold.

135
00:05:37,772 --> 00:05:40,675
And at resale, a buyer may still cut a little.

136
00:05:40,675 --> 00:05:42,417
Smaller leak, but still a leak.

137
00:05:42,417 --> 00:05:45,389
Then, a gold ETF. ETF means exchange traded fund.

138
00:05:45,389 --> 00:05:50,013
It is a fund that buys real gold and splits it into small units.

139
00:05:50,013 --> 00:05:53,196
You buy these units on the stock market, like a share.

140
00:05:53,196 --> 00:05:56,091
You need a demat account, your online locker for shares.

141
00:05:56,091 --> 00:05:57,827
Each unit follows the gold price.

142
00:05:57,827 --> 00:05:59,454
Here is the big difference.

143
00:05:59,454 --> 00:06:01,731
There is no making charge at all.

144
00:06:01,731 --> 00:06:04,985
And buying gold ETF units attracts no GST, as reported.

145
00:06:04,985 --> 00:06:06,612
No locker, no theft worry.

146
00:06:06,612 --> 00:06:08,479
So what does an ETF cost?

147
00:06:08,479 --> 00:06:10,967
A small yearly fee called the expense ratio.

148
00:06:10,967 --> 00:06:15,323
One large gold ETF reported a fee of 0.68 percent for a recent year.

149
00:06:15,323 --> 00:06:18,335
Expense ratio simply means the fund's yearly running fee,

150
00:06:18,335 --> 00:06:20,343
taken quietly from your money. Small,

151
00:06:20,343 --> 00:06:22,685
but it adds up over many years.

152
00:06:22,685 --> 00:06:23,797
And selling is simple.

153
00:06:23,797 --> 00:06:26,576
You sell units on the market near the gold price.

154
00:06:26,576 --> 00:06:29,355
No weighing, no testing, no purity argument at the counter.

155
00:06:29,355 --> 00:06:31,129
There may be a small brokerage,

156
00:06:31,129 --> 00:06:33,200
which is your broker's fee per trade.

157
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:36,157
But it is nothing like losing a whole making charge.

158
00:06:36,157 --> 00:06:39,488
So which one wins? It depends on why you buy.

159
00:06:39,488 --> 00:06:42,153
For wearing and tradition, jewellery. For pure saving,

160
00:06:42,153 --> 00:06:43,818
the leak matters a lot.

161
00:06:43,818 --> 00:06:46,261
But even smart savers make one common mistake.

162
00:06:46,261 --> 00:06:48,704
It is not about the shop at all.

163
00:06:48,704 --> 00:06:51,452
It is about timing and tax. Let us see.

164
00:06:51,452 --> 00:06:53,932
Sell physical gold, jewellery or coins, at a profit,

165
00:06:53,932 --> 00:06:54,758
and tax applies.

166
00:06:54,758 --> 00:06:57,789
Hold it at least 24 months to count as long term.

167
00:06:57,789 --> 00:07:01,212
Long term profit on gold is taxed at 12.5 percent,

168
00:07:01,212 --> 00:07:04,977
without indexation. Indexation means raising your cost for rising prices. Here,

169
00:07:04,977 --> 00:07:06,689
you do not get that.

170
00:07:06,689 --> 00:07:09,194
Now the mistake. A family buys jewellery as savings.

171
00:07:09,194 --> 00:07:11,142
Then they sell it in a hurry.

172
00:07:11,142 --> 00:07:12,534
They lose the making charge,

173
00:07:12,534 --> 00:07:15,039
and any profit is taxed at their income slab.

174
00:07:15,039 --> 00:07:19,184
The real mistake is mixing beauty and savings in a single purchase.

175
00:07:19,184 --> 00:07:20,911
Jewellery is great for wearing.

176
00:07:20,911 --> 00:07:23,329
It is a costly way to save.

177
00:07:23,329 --> 00:07:25,577
So what can you do? Before you buy,

178
00:07:25,577 --> 00:07:29,793
ask the jeweller to write the making charge as a separate line on the bill.

179
00:07:29,793 --> 00:07:31,868
Ask for the buyback terms in writing.

180
00:07:31,868 --> 00:07:34,833
How much will the shop deduct if you sell later?

181
00:07:34,833 --> 00:07:36,315
A good jeweller answers clearly.

182
00:07:36,315 --> 00:07:40,041
Check the hallmark and the HUID on the BIS Care app.

183
00:07:40,041 --> 00:07:42,073
If you only want to save,

184
00:07:42,073 --> 00:07:44,105
compare coins and ETFs before jewellery.

185
00:07:44,105 --> 00:07:47,594
Before selling physical gold, check if you have crossed 24 months.

186
00:07:47,594 --> 00:07:49,180
Rules and rates can change,

187
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:51,401
so check the latest before you act.

188
00:07:51,401 --> 00:07:54,733
This is not investment advice. It is simply your bill,

189
00:07:54,733 --> 00:07:58,397
explained line by line, so you know where every rupee goes.

190
00:07:58,397 --> 00:07:59,730
Your choice, your reasons.

191
00:07:59,730 --> 00:08:02,628
So, you paid for gold. How much gold came home?

192
00:08:02,628 --> 00:08:04,656
Only the gold line on your bill.

193
00:08:04,656 --> 00:08:06,685
Everything else was the price of beauty.

194
00:08:06,685 --> 00:08:08,077
Send this to your mother,

195
00:08:08,077 --> 00:08:10,304
who is planning the wedding jewellery this season.

196
00:08:10,304 --> 00:08:13,087
Let her read every line of the bill before paying.

197
00:08:13,087 --> 00:08:18,358
Now tell me in the comments. In your family,

198
00:08:18,358 --> 00:08:23,629
is gold for wearing or for saving? Next time,

199
00:08:23,629 --> 00:08:28,900
we decode another bill most of us never read.
